From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pet0203 <Pet0203@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tlcl: add TPM 2.0 policy sessions and a policy-authorised NV
 read

tpm2_lite authorises everything with a password session: the proof is an
empty password, and whatever comes back off the bus is taken as the TPM's
answer. That is not enough to release disk keys from an NV index that is
defined with policyread and no authread, where satisfying the index's
policy is the only way in and the PIN that satisfies it should not cross
the bus.

Add the four commands a policy session needs (StartAuthSession, PolicyPCR,
PolicyAuthValue, FlushContext) and TlclReadPolicy(), an NV_Read authorised
by that session instead of by a password.

The session is unsalted and unbound on purpose. That leaves its session key
empty, so the HMAC key is the index's authValue alone, which the caller
derives from the PIN. Proving knowledge of it does not reveal it, and a
salted session would need asymmetric crypto this library does not carry.

The response HMAC is verified, not just the command one, so a reply that
did not come from the TPM is rejected.

What this does NOT give is confidentiality of the data read. The session
uses no parameter encryption, so the NV contents cross the bus in the
clear: anything able to observe the TPM's bus during the read sees them.
Closing that needs an encrypted (salted) session, which is not implemented.

Details that are easy to get wrong, and silent when wrong:

 - Both kinds of NV_Read share a command code, so the marshaller cannot
   tell from the code which body it was handed, and the response parser
   cannot tell which authorisation section to expect: a password session's
   is five bytes, a policy session's is sixty-nine and carries the proof.
   tpm_set_nv_read_policy() selects one for the duration of the exchange,
   following tpm_set_ph_disabled(), which exists for the same reason.

 - cpHash covers the Name of both handles, and for NV_Read both handles
   are the index, so its Name is hashed twice. The Name is taken from
   NV_ReadPublic rather than recomputed.

 - tlcl.h is shared with TPM 1.2 builds and must not name TPM2-only types,
   so the new declarations use uint32_t rather than TPM_HANDLE.

 - TlclGetRandom() reports a short read rather than failing, and the Cr50
   routinely returns fewer bytes than asked for. The session nonces are
   therefore gathered in a loop.

 - TPM_BUFFER_SIZE grows from 256 to 1024 bytes so that a 780-byte key
   block fits in one exchange (the Cr50's TPM2_PT_INPUT_BUFFER is 0x400).
   It costs 768 bytes of BSS: the TPM2B structures hold pointers into that
   buffer rather than copies.

 - The read's length check is written out. sizeof() of the TPM2B's buffer
   member is the size of a pointer here, which would cap every read at 8
   bytes.

 - tlcl.a gets its own copy of 2hmac.c. hmac() lives in the firmware
   library, which the linker searches before tlcl.a, so nothing had pulled
   the object in by the time tlcl.o needed it. An archive member is only
   taken to resolve a still-undefined symbol, so the two never collide.

Exercised against a Cr50 (H1, RW 0.5.241) on google,spherion: the policy
digest, both HMACs and a single 780-byte read.
---
 Makefile                              |   3 +-
 firmware/include/tlcl.h               |  25 +++
 firmware/include/tpm2_marshaling.h    |  10 +
 firmware/include/tpm2_tss_constants.h |  69 ++++++-
 firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c   | 153 ++++++++++++++-
 firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/tlcl.c         | 268 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 6 files changed, 524 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 5f5132c..b5b48fa 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -426,7 +426,8 @@ else
 # TODO(apronin): tests for TPM2 case?
 TLCL_SRCS = \
 	firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/tlcl.c \
-	firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c
+	firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c \
+	firmware/2lib/2hmac.c
 endif
 
 # Support real TPM unless MOCK_TPM is set
diff --git a/firmware/include/tlcl.h b/firmware/include/tlcl.h
index d33fa47..32ca100 100644
--- a/firmware/include/tlcl.h
+++ b/firmware/include/tlcl.h
@@ -119,6 +119,31 @@ uint32_t TlclWrite(uint32_t index, const void *data, uint32_t length);
  */
 uint32_t TlclRead(uint32_t index, void *data, uint32_t length);
 
+/**
+ * Policy sessions, for reading an NV index sealed to PCR values and a PIN.
+ *
+ * The index the disk keys live in is defined with policyread and no authread,
+ * so it cannot be read with a password at all: satisfying its policy is the
+ * only way in. TlclStartAuthSession opens the session and returns the TPM's
+ * nonce, TlclPolicyPCR and TlclPolicyAuthValue drive the policy to the value
+ * the index was sealed under, and TlclReadPolicy performs the authorized read
+ * and checks the HMAC on the way back. TlclFlushContext releases the session;
+ * the TPM holds only a few, so leaking them wedges later boots.
+ *
+ * |nonce_tpm| is SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE bytes, carried from TlclStartAuthSession
+ * into TlclReadPolicy, which rolls it forward. |auth| is the index's authValue,
+ * which the caller derives from the PIN -- it is the HMAC key and never
+ * appears on the bus.
+ */
+uint32_t TlclStartAuthSession(uint32_t *session_handle, uint8_t *nonce_tpm);
+uint32_t TlclPolicyPCR(uint32_t session_handle,
+		       uint32_t pcr_selection_bitmap);
+uint32_t TlclPolicyAuthValue(uint32_t session_handle);
+uint32_t TlclReadPolicy(uint32_t index, void *data, uint32_t length,
+			uint32_t session_handle, uint8_t *nonce_tpm,
+			const uint8_t *auth, uint32_t auth_size);
+uint32_t TlclFlushContext(uint32_t handle);
+
 /**
  * Read PCR at [index] into [data].  [length] must be TPM_PCR_DIGEST or
  * larger. The TPM error code is returned.
diff --git a/firmware/include/tpm2_marshaling.h b/firmware/include/tpm2_marshaling.h
index 9fd79da..1d69204 100644
--- a/firmware/include/tpm2_marshaling.h
+++ b/firmware/include/tpm2_marshaling.h
@@ -79,6 +79,16 @@ uint32_t tpm_get_packet_response_code(const uint8_t *packet);
  */
 void tpm_set_ph_disabled(int flag);
 
+/**
+ * tpm_set_nv_read_policy
+ *
+ * A policy-authorized NV_Read and a password one share a command code, so the
+ * marshaller cannot tell from the code alone which body it was given. The
+ * caller sets this for the duration of the exchange; the response parser reads
+ * it too, because the authorization section differs in size and meaning.
+ */
+void tpm_set_nv_read_policy(int flag);
+
 /**
  * tpm_is_ph_disabled
  *
diff --git a/firmware/include/tpm2_tss_constants.h b/firmware/include/tpm2_tss_constants.h
index becd710..0e9e868 100644
--- a/firmware/include/tpm2_tss_constants.h
+++ b/firmware/include/tpm2_tss_constants.h
@@ -13,7 +13,14 @@
 extern "C" {
 #endif  /* __cplusplus */
 
-#define TPM_BUFFER_SIZE 256
+/*
+ * Big enough for the firmware unlock's key block in one exchange. vboot's own
+ * NV spaces are tens of bytes, so 256 was plenty; the disk keys are 780, and
+ * the Cr50 hands all of them back in a single NV_Read (its own
+ * TPM2_PT_INPUT_BUFFER is 0x400). The cost is 768 bytes of BSS: the TPM2B
+ * structures hold pointers into this buffer rather than copies.
+ */
+#define TPM_BUFFER_SIZE 1024
 
 /* Tpm2 command tags. */
 #define TPM_ST_NO_SESSIONS 0x8001
@@ -35,6 +42,13 @@ extern "C" {
 #define TPM2_GetCapability     ((TPM_CC)0x0000017A)
 #define TPM2_GetRandom         ((TPM_CC)0x0000017B)
 #define TPM2_PCR_Extend        ((TPM_CC)0x00000182)
+/* Policy sessions. The unlock reads an NV index whose authPolicy is
+ * PolicyPCR + PolicyAuthValue, which needs a real session rather than the
+ * password sessions the rest of this file uses. */
+#define TPM2_StartAuthSession  ((TPM_CC)0x00000176)
+#define TPM2_PolicyPCR         ((TPM_CC)0x0000017F)
+#define TPM2_PolicyAuthValue   ((TPM_CC)0x0000016B)
+#define TPM2_FlushContext      ((TPM_CC)0x00000165)
 
 #define TPM_HT_PCR             0x00
 #define TPM_HT_NV_INDEX        0x01
@@ -45,6 +59,9 @@ extern "C" {
 #define TPM_RH_OWNER        0x40000001
 #define TPM_RH_PLATFORM     0x4000000C
 #define TPM_RS_PW           0x40000009
+#define TPM_RH_NULL         0x40000007
+/* TPM_SE_POLICY: a session whose authorization is a policy, not a password. */
+#define TPM_SE_POLICY       ((uint8_t)0x01)
 
 /* TPM2 capabilities. */
 #define TPM_CAP_FIRST                   ((TPM_CAP)0x00000000)
@@ -252,6 +269,42 @@ struct tpm2_pcr_extend_cmd {
 	TPML_DIGEST_VALUES digests;
 };
 
+/*
+ * The session is started unsalted (tpmKey = TPM_RH_NULL) and unbound
+ * (bind = TPM_RH_NULL). That is deliberate: it makes the session key empty,
+ * so the HMAC key is the NV index's authValue on its own, which is the one
+ * secret the firmware derives from the PIN. A salted session would need
+ * asymmetric work this library does not have.
+ */
+struct tpm2_start_auth_session_cmd {
+	uint8_t nonce_caller[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+};
+
+struct tpm2_policy_pcr_cmd {
+	TPM_HANDLE session_handle;
+	/* One bit per PCR, matching the TPML_PCR_SELECTION bitmap. */
+	uint32_t pcr_selection_bitmap;
+};
+
+struct tpm2_policy_authvalue_cmd {
+	TPM_HANDLE session_handle;
+};
+
+struct tpm2_flush_context_cmd {
+	TPM_HANDLE flush_handle;
+};
+
+/* NV_Read authorized by a policy session instead of an empty password. */
+struct tpm2_nv_read_policy_cmd {
+	TPMI_RH_NV_INDEX nvIndex;
+	uint16_t size;
+	uint16_t offset;
+	TPM_HANDLE session_handle;
+	uint8_t nonce_caller[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	uint8_t session_attrs;
+	uint8_t hmac[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+};
+
 /* Common command/response header. */
 struct tpm_header {
 	uint16_t tpm_tag;
@@ -262,6 +315,19 @@ struct tpm_header {
 struct nv_read_response {
 	uint32_t params_size;
 	TPM2B_MAX_NV_BUFFER buffer;
+	/*
+	 * Only meaningful for a read authorized by a session: the response
+	 * HMAC is what proves the answer came from the TPM rather than from
+	 * whatever else can reach the bus.
+	 */
+	TPM2B_DIGEST nonce_tpm;
+	uint8_t session_attrs;
+	TPM2B_DIGEST hmac;
+};
+
+struct start_auth_session_response {
+	TPM_HANDLE session_handle;
+	TPM2B_DIGEST nonce_tpm;
 };
 
 struct tpm2_session_attrs {
@@ -308,6 +374,7 @@ struct tpm2_response {
 		struct get_capability_response cap;
 		struct get_random_response random;
 		struct nv_read_public_response nv_read_public;
+		struct start_auth_session_response auth_session;
 	};
 };
 
diff --git a/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c b/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c
index b9857d4..fd820ba 100644
--- a/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c
+++ b/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/marshaling.c
@@ -8,6 +8,14 @@
 #include "tpm2_marshaling.h"
 
 static uint16_t tpm_tag;  /* Depends on the command type. */
+
+/*
+ * Set while marshaling an NV_Read authorized by a policy session. The response
+ * parser has to know which shape to expect: a password session's authorization
+ * section is five bytes and carries nothing, while a policy session's holds the
+ * TPM nonce and the HMAC that prove the answer came from the TPM.
+ */
+static int nv_read_session_auth;
 static int ph_disabled;   /* Platform hierarchy disabled. */
 
 static void write_be16(void *dest, uint16_t val)
@@ -136,6 +144,16 @@ static void unmarshal_authorization_section(void **buffer, int *size,
 	*size = 0;
 }
 
+static void unmarshal_TPM2B(void **buffer, int *size, TPM2B *tpm2b);
+
+static void unmarshal_session_authorization(void **buffer, int *size,
+					    struct nv_read_response *nvr)
+{
+	unmarshal_TPM2B(buffer, size, &nvr->nonce_tpm);
+	nvr->session_attrs = unmarshal_u8(buffer, size);
+	unmarshal_TPM2B(buffer, size, &nvr->hmac);
+}
+
 static void unmarshal_nv_read(void **buffer, int *size,
 			      struct nv_read_response *nvr)
 {
@@ -153,7 +171,10 @@ static void unmarshal_nv_read(void **buffer, int *size,
 	if (*size < 0)
 		return;
 
-	unmarshal_authorization_section(buffer, size, "NV_Read");
+	if (nv_read_session_auth)
+		unmarshal_session_authorization(buffer, size, nvr);
+	else
+		unmarshal_authorization_section(buffer, size, "NV_Read");
 }
 
 static void unmarshal_TPM2B(void **buffer,
@@ -200,6 +221,18 @@ static void unmarshal_TPMS_NV_PUBLIC(void **buffer,
 	}
 }
 
+static void unmarshal_start_auth_session(void **buffer, int *size,
+					 struct start_auth_session_response *r)
+{
+	r->session_handle = unmarshal_u32(buffer, size);
+	unmarshal_TPM2B(buffer, size, &r->nonce_tpm);
+
+	if (*size > 0) {
+		VB2_DEBUG("extra %d bytes after nonceTPM\n", *size);
+		*size = -1;
+	}
+}
+
 static void unmarshal_nv_read_public(void **buffer, int *size,
 				     struct nv_read_public_response *nv_pub)
 {
@@ -555,6 +588,83 @@ static void marshal_nv_read(void **buffer,
 	marshal_u16(buffer, command_body->offset, buffer_space);
 }
 
+static void marshal_start_auth_session(void **buffer,
+				      struct tpm2_start_auth_session_cmd *cmd,
+				      int *buffer_space)
+{
+	/* Unsalted and unbound: both key handles are TPM_RH_NULL. */
+	marshal_TPM_HANDLE(buffer, TPM_RH_NULL, buffer_space);
+	marshal_TPM_HANDLE(buffer, TPM_RH_NULL, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u16(buffer, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE, buffer_space);
+	marshal_blob(buffer, cmd->nonce_caller, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE,
+		     buffer_space);
+	marshal_u16(buffer, 0, buffer_space);	/* no encrypted salt */
+	marshal_u8(buffer, TPM_SE_POLICY, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u16(buffer, TPM_ALG_NULL, buffer_space);   /* no parameter encryption */
+	marshal_u16(buffer, TPM_ALG_SHA256, buffer_space);
+}
+
+static void marshal_policy_pcr(void **buffer,
+			       struct tpm2_policy_pcr_cmd *cmd,
+			       int *buffer_space)
+{
+	uint32_t bitmap = cmd->pcr_selection_bitmap;
+
+	marshal_TPM_HANDLE(buffer, cmd->session_handle, buffer_space);
+	/*
+	 * An empty pcrDigest tells the TPM to use the PCRs as they are now,
+	 * which is what we want: the policy either matches this boot or it
+	 * does not.
+	 */
+	marshal_u16(buffer, 0, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u32(buffer, 1, buffer_space);		/* one selection */
+	marshal_u16(buffer, TPM_ALG_SHA256, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u8(buffer, 3, buffer_space);		/* three bitmap bytes */
+	marshal_u8(buffer, bitmap & 0xff, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u8(buffer, (bitmap >> 8) & 0xff, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u8(buffer, (bitmap >> 16) & 0xff, buffer_space);
+}
+
+static void marshal_policy_authvalue(void **buffer,
+				     struct tpm2_policy_authvalue_cmd *cmd,
+				     int *buffer_space)
+{
+	marshal_TPM_HANDLE(buffer, cmd->session_handle, buffer_space);
+}
+
+static void marshal_flush_context(void **buffer,
+				  struct tpm2_flush_context_cmd *cmd,
+				  int *buffer_space)
+{
+	/* FlushContext takes its handle in the parameter area, not as a
+	 * handle, so it is marshalled like any other u32. */
+	marshal_u32(buffer, cmd->flush_handle, buffer_space);
+}
+
+static void marshal_nv_read_policy(void **buffer,
+				   struct tpm2_nv_read_policy_cmd *cmd,
+				   int *buffer_space)
+{
+	struct tpm2_session_header session_header;
+
+	/* The index authorizes itself: its policy is what is being satisfied. */
+	marshal_TPM_HANDLE(buffer, cmd->nvIndex, buffer_space);
+	marshal_TPM_HANDLE(buffer, cmd->nvIndex, buffer_space);
+
+	memset(&session_header, 0, sizeof(session_header));
+	session_header.session_handle = cmd->session_handle;
+	session_header.nonce_size = SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE;
+	session_header.nonce = cmd->nonce_caller;
+	session_header.session_attrs = cmd->session_attrs;
+	session_header.auth_size = SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE;
+	session_header.auth = cmd->hmac;
+	marshal_session_header(buffer, &session_header, buffer_space);
+	tpm_tag = TPM_ST_SESSIONS;
+
+	marshal_u16(buffer, cmd->size, buffer_space);
+	marshal_u16(buffer, cmd->offset, buffer_space);
+}
+
 static void marshal_nv_read_lock(void **buffer,
 				  struct tpm2_nv_read_lock_cmd *command_body,
 				  int *buffer_space)
@@ -735,7 +845,30 @@ int tpm_marshal_command(TPM_CC command, void *tpm_command_body,
 		break;
 
 	case TPM2_NV_Read:
-		marshal_nv_read(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body, &body_size);
+		if (nv_read_session_auth)
+			marshal_nv_read_policy(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body,
+					       &body_size);
+		else
+			marshal_nv_read(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body,
+					&body_size);
+		break;
+
+	case TPM2_StartAuthSession:
+		marshal_start_auth_session(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body,
+					   &body_size);
+		break;
+
+	case TPM2_PolicyPCR:
+		marshal_policy_pcr(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body, &body_size);
+		break;
+
+	case TPM2_PolicyAuthValue:
+		marshal_policy_authvalue(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body,
+					 &body_size);
+		break;
+
+	case TPM2_FlushContext:
+		marshal_flush_context(&cmd_body, tpm_command_body, &body_size);
 		break;
 
 	case TPM2_NV_Write:
@@ -838,6 +971,17 @@ int tpm_unmarshal_response(TPM_CC command,
 				         &response->nv_read_public);
 		break;
 
+	case TPM2_StartAuthSession:
+		unmarshal_start_auth_session(&response_body, &cr_size,
+					     &response->auth_session);
+		break;
+
+	case TPM2_PolicyPCR:
+	case TPM2_PolicyAuthValue:
+	case TPM2_FlushContext:
+		/* These return nothing beyond the response code. */
+		break;
+
 	case TPM2_GetCapability:
 		unmarshal_get_capability(&response_body, &cr_size,
 					 &response->cap);
@@ -917,6 +1061,11 @@ void tpm_set_ph_disabled(int flag)
 	ph_disabled = flag;
 }
 
+void tpm_set_nv_read_policy(int flag)
+{
+	nv_read_session_auth = flag;
+}
+
 int tpm_is_ph_disabled(void)
 {
 	return ph_disabled;
diff --git a/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/tlcl.c b/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/tlcl.c
index b91119b..95ac246 100644
--- a/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/tlcl.c
+++ b/firmware/lib/tpm2_lite/tlcl.c
@@ -9,6 +9,8 @@
 #include "2common.h"
 #include "2sysincludes.h"
 #include "tlcl.h"
+#include "2hmac.h"
+#include "2sha.h"
 #include "tpm2_marshaling.h"
 
 /*
@@ -683,3 +685,269 @@ uint32_t TlclIFXFieldUpgradeInfo(TPM_IFX_FIELDUPGRADEINFO* info)
 	VB2_DEBUG("NOT YET IMPLEMENTED\n");
 	return TPM_E_IOERROR;
 }
+
+/*
+ * Policy sessions, for the firmware unlock.
+ *
+ * Everything above authorizes with a password session: the proof is an empty
+ * password and the TPM's answer is taken on faith. Neither is good enough for
+ * the disk keys. The PIN must not cross the bus, and the reply has to be
+ * provably the TPM's rather than whatever else can reach the SPI lines. Both
+ * follow from an HMAC session. Because the session is unsalted and unbound its
+ * session key is empty, which leaves the index's authValue as the whole HMAC
+ * key -- and that authValue is derived from the PIN, so proving knowledge of it
+ * never reveals it.
+ */
+
+/* cpHash = H(commandCode || names of both handles || parameters). Both handles
+ * of NV_Read are the index itself, so its Name is hashed twice. */
+static uint32_t nv_read_cp_hash(const uint8_t *name, uint16_t name_size,
+				uint16_t size, uint16_t offset, uint8_t *out)
+{
+	struct vb2_digest_context dc;
+	uint8_t be[4];
+
+	if (vb2_digest_init(&dc, VB2_HASH_SHA256) != VB2_SUCCESS)
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+
+	be[0] = (TPM2_NV_Read >> 24) & 0xff;
+	be[1] = (TPM2_NV_Read >> 16) & 0xff;
+	be[2] = (TPM2_NV_Read >> 8) & 0xff;
+	be[3] = TPM2_NV_Read & 0xff;
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, be, sizeof(be));
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, name, name_size);
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, name, name_size);
+
+	be[0] = (size >> 8) & 0xff;
+	be[1] = size & 0xff;
+	be[2] = (offset >> 8) & 0xff;
+	be[3] = offset & 0xff;
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, be, sizeof(be));
+
+	if (vb2_digest_finalize(&dc, out, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE) != VB2_SUCCESS)
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+
+	return TPM_SUCCESS;
+}
+
+/* The HMAC covers the command or response hash and both nonces, which is what
+ * stops a recorded exchange being replayed. */
+static uint32_t session_hmac(const uint8_t *auth, uint32_t auth_size,
+			     const uint8_t *hash, const uint8_t *nonce_newer,
+			     const uint8_t *nonce_older, uint8_t attrs,
+			     uint8_t *out)
+{
+	uint8_t msg[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE * 3 + 1];
+
+	memcpy(msg, hash, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+	memcpy(msg + SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE, nonce_newer, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+	memcpy(msg + SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE * 2, nonce_older, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+	msg[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE * 3] = attrs;
+
+	if (hmac(VB2_HASH_SHA256, auth, auth_size, msg, sizeof(msg),
+		 out, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE))
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+
+	return TPM_SUCCESS;
+}
+
+/*
+ * A full-length nonce, however many calls it takes.
+ *
+ * TlclGetRandom reports a short read instead of failing -- it only errors when
+ * the TPM returns MORE than was asked for -- and the Cr50 routinely returns
+ * less. Treating that as fatal is what made the first firmware unlock fail
+ * with TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR before it had sent a single session command.
+ */
+static uint32_t session_nonce(uint8_t *out)
+{
+	uint32_t got = 0, size, rv;
+	int tries;
+
+	for (tries = 0; tries < 8 && got < SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE; tries++) {
+		size = 0;
+		rv = TlclGetRandom(out + got, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE - got, &size);
+		if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+			return rv;
+		if (size == 0)
+			break;
+		got += size;
+	}
+
+	if (got != SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE) {
+		VB2_DEBUG("only got %u of %u nonce bytes\n",
+			  got, (uint32_t)SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+	}
+
+	return TPM_SUCCESS;
+}
+
+uint32_t TlclStartAuthSession(uint32_t *session_handle, uint8_t *nonce_tpm)
+{
+	struct tpm2_response *response = &tpm2_resp;
+	struct tpm2_start_auth_session_cmd cmd;
+	uint32_t rv;
+
+	memset(&cmd, 0, sizeof(cmd));
+	/* A fresh nonceCaller per session; the TPM answers with its own. */
+	rv = session_nonce(cmd.nonce_caller);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	rv = tpm_send_receive(TPM2_StartAuthSession, &cmd, response);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	if (response->auth_session.nonce_tpm.size != SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE)
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+
+	*session_handle = response->auth_session.session_handle;
+	memcpy(nonce_tpm, response->auth_session.nonce_tpm.buffer,
+	       SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+
+	return TPM_SUCCESS;
+}
+
+uint32_t TlclPolicyPCR(uint32_t session_handle, uint32_t pcr_selection_bitmap)
+{
+	struct tpm2_policy_pcr_cmd cmd;
+
+	cmd.session_handle = session_handle;
+	cmd.pcr_selection_bitmap = pcr_selection_bitmap;
+
+	return tpm_get_response_code(TPM2_PolicyPCR, &cmd);
+}
+
+uint32_t TlclPolicyAuthValue(uint32_t session_handle)
+{
+	struct tpm2_policy_authvalue_cmd cmd;
+
+	cmd.session_handle = session_handle;
+
+	return tpm_get_response_code(TPM2_PolicyAuthValue, &cmd);
+}
+
+uint32_t TlclFlushContext(uint32_t handle)
+{
+	struct tpm2_flush_context_cmd cmd;
+
+	cmd.flush_handle = handle;
+
+	return tpm_get_response_code(TPM2_FlushContext, &cmd);
+}
+
+uint32_t TlclReadPolicy(uint32_t index, void *data, uint32_t length,
+			uint32_t session_handle, uint8_t *nonce_tpm,
+			const uint8_t *auth, uint32_t auth_size)
+{
+	struct tpm2_response *response = &tpm2_resp;
+	struct nv_read_public_response *nv_public;
+	struct tpm2_nv_read_policy_cmd cmd;
+	uint8_t name[sizeof(TPMU_HA) + sizeof(uint16_t)];
+	uint8_t cp_hash[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	uint8_t rp_hash[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	uint8_t expected[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+	struct vb2_digest_context dc;
+	uint16_t name_size;
+	uint8_t be[4];
+	uint32_t rv;
+
+	/*
+	 * The whole response has to fit in TLCL's command buffer: the header,
+	 * the parameter size, the data's own size field, and the session's
+	 * nonce, attributes and HMAC -- 85 bytes of it, rounded up here.
+	 *
+	 * The bound used to be sizeof(...->t.buffer), which in this TPM2B is a
+	 * POINTER into the response buffer rather than an array. That made the
+	 * limit 8 bytes and refused every read worth doing, with
+	 * TPM_E_BUFFER_SIZE and no hint as to why.
+	 */
+	if (length > TPM_BUFFER_SIZE - 128)
+		return TPM_E_BUFFER_SIZE;
+
+	/* The Name goes into cpHash. Ask the TPM for it rather than
+	 * recomputing it from the public area and risking disagreement. */
+	rv = tlcl_nv_read_public(index, &nv_public);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+	name_size = nv_public->nvName.size;
+	if (name_size > sizeof(name))
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+	memcpy(name, nv_public->nvName.buffer, name_size);
+
+	memset(&cmd, 0, sizeof(cmd));
+	cmd.nvIndex = HR_NV_INDEX + index;
+	cmd.size = length;
+	cmd.offset = 0;
+	cmd.session_handle = session_handle;
+	cmd.session_attrs = 1;	/* continueSession: we flush it ourselves */
+
+	/* A fresh nonce for this command, or the HMAC could be replayed. */
+	rv = session_nonce(cmd.nonce_caller);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	rv = nv_read_cp_hash(name, name_size, cmd.size, cmd.offset, cp_hash);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	rv = session_hmac(auth, auth_size, cp_hash, cmd.nonce_caller,
+			  nonce_tpm, cmd.session_attrs, cmd.hmac);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	/* Both reads are TPM2_NV_Read on the wire, so the marshaller is told
+	 * which body it has been handed. Cleared again below, including on the
+	 * failure paths, or the next password read is misparsed. */
+	tpm_set_nv_read_policy(1);
+	rv = tpm_send_receive(TPM2_NV_Read, &cmd, response);
+	tpm_set_nv_read_policy(0);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	if (response->nvr.buffer.t.size != length)
+		return TPM_E_RESPONSE_TOO_LARGE;
+	if (response->nvr.nonce_tpm.size != SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE ||
+	    response->nvr.hmac.size != SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE)
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+
+	/* rpHash = H(responseCode || commandCode || responseParameters).
+	 * Verifying it is what makes the answer the TPM's rather than the
+	 * bus's. */
+	if (vb2_digest_init(&dc, VB2_HASH_SHA256) != VB2_SUCCESS)
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+	memset(be, 0, sizeof(be));			/* TPM_RC_SUCCESS */
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, be, sizeof(be));
+	be[0] = (TPM2_NV_Read >> 24) & 0xff;
+	be[1] = (TPM2_NV_Read >> 16) & 0xff;
+	be[2] = (TPM2_NV_Read >> 8) & 0xff;
+	be[3] = TPM2_NV_Read & 0xff;
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, be, sizeof(be));
+	be[0] = (response->nvr.buffer.t.size >> 8) & 0xff;
+	be[1] = response->nvr.buffer.t.size & 0xff;
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, be, 2);
+	vb2_digest_extend(&dc, response->nvr.buffer.t.buffer,
+			  response->nvr.buffer.t.size);
+	if (vb2_digest_finalize(&dc, rp_hash, sizeof(rp_hash)) != VB2_SUCCESS)
+		return TPM_E_INTERNAL_ERROR;
+
+	rv = session_hmac(auth, auth_size, rp_hash,
+			  response->nvr.nonce_tpm.buffer, cmd.nonce_caller,
+			  response->nvr.session_attrs, expected);
+	if (rv != TPM_SUCCESS)
+		return rv;
+
+	if (vb2_safe_memcmp(expected, response->nvr.hmac.buffer,
+			    SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE)) {
+		VB2_DEBUG("NV_Read response HMAC mismatch\n");
+		return TPM_E_AUTHFAIL;
+	}
+
+	memcpy(data, response->nvr.buffer.t.buffer, length);
+
+	/* The TPM's nonce rolls forward for any later use of this session. */
+	memcpy(nonce_tpm, response->nvr.nonce_tpm.buffer, SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE);
+
+	return TPM_SUCCESS;
+}
